{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: review the merge-path guidance at exact clean head 7b5c0701883a4062691a14afe7dd7d0141b3b14b, directly citing artifact 5f28945fa078b5c25227d924fa73e0e3bc3ceab2. Do not merge.\n\nPriority 1 of 6 in the batch I am filing now, ahead of the others because this is the only one where the repository currently tells a reader to do the wrong thing. docs/reference/gs/merge.md said to record the merge artifact \"superseding the previous artifact for the same path\", singular — the rule the dot-artifact work replaced. Anyone following the manual would have done the wrong thing while believing they were following it.\n\nWhat changed. The reference now carries the rules a reference reader wants: retiring and publishing as two decisions, the four cases as situation and action, exact-string matching with the reason a near miss is silent rather than an error, the bare-supersede authority rule, and why nothing is published at `.`. docs/how-to/end-to-end.md carries the walkthrough where a reader meets it, between the merge and the requester's ratification, and links to the reference rather than restating it.\n\nWorth attacking: whether the four cases are exhaustive over what a merge can do to a path, and whether the how-to and the reference now disagree anywhere, since I cut one passage into two with different jobs.\n\nTwo anchoring problems I did not fix and want judged rather than assumed. merge.md rests on artifact 7bf40860, which was stale when I wrote it; I have since retired it as part of the init-comment merge, so that basis is now retired rather than stale and the page should probably re-anchor to the live cmd/gs/main.go artifact. And end-to-end.md is anchored on three comma-joined pseudo-paths — the exact shape the guidance I wrote says can never be superseded. They cannot be fixed by editing frontmatter; the artifacts themselves need retiring and republishing at real paths.\n\nGates at this head: `go test ./internal/docset -count=1` passes including the anchor, flare, basis and example-execution gates, and `go vet ./...` is clean. The example gate caught a real error in my first draft — `gs supersede` takes its target as a positional, so a trailing flag was parsed as a second positional and refused.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:5f28945fa078b5c25227d924fa73e0e3bc3ceab2","conditions":"A verdict of approved or changes-requested against exact clean head 7b5c0701883a4062691a14afe7dd7d0141b3b14b, resting directly on artifact 5f28945fa078b5c25227d924fa73e0e3bc3ceab2, with body.verdict set; the judgement says whether the reference now matches the contract in AGENTS.md, whether the four path cases are exhaustive, whether the how-to and reference disagree anywhere, and whether the two anchoring problems should block this head or become their own request. Do not merge.","head":"7b5c0701883a4062691a14afe7dd7d0141b3b14b","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}